Let's compute the cross product of the standard basis vectors from R³ . 
First consider i x j as shown in FIgure 1.53. 
The vectors i and j determine a square of unit area. 
Thus, ||i x j|| = 1. 
Any vector perpendicular to both i and j must be perpendicular to the plane in which i and j lie. 
Hence, i x j must point in the direction of + or - k. 
The "right-hand rule" implies that i x j must point in the positive k direction.
Since ||k|| = 1, we conclude that i x j = k. 
The same argument establishes that j x k = i and k x i = j. 
To remember these basic equations, you  can draw i, j, and k in a circle, as in figure 1.54. 
Then the relations: 

		i x j = k, j x k = i, k x i = j

may be read from the circle by beginning at any vector and then proceeding clockwise. 
